  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Between 2022 and 2023 the Serbian Association of Employers (SAE) conducted a comprehensive assessment of skills needs anticipation based on the ILO &#x201c;Skills for Trade and Economic Diversification&#x201d; (STED) methodology. In an assessment of the business environment conducted by the SAE in 2021, a lack of adequate skills among the young and adult workforce featured as one of the most critical obstacles to enterprise development.

With a focus on the hotel industry, which has a promising growth potential, the SAE resolved to address this challenge by carrying out a diagnostic study to anticipate future demand for trained professionals and address skills deficits by developing a skills strategy for the hotel industry. The diagnostic background study was carried out in collaboration with competent consultants and encompassed a desk review, qualitative analysis and survey data which gathered the views of 269 respondents between Nov 2022-Jan 2023. 

Findings of the diagnostic study were discussed at a foresight workshop on 12-13 Sep 2023 and served as the basis for identifying priority areas of intervention for meeting the future skills demand of the hotel industry. A very large and diverse range of stakeholders participated in the workshop, including companies and business associations in the hotel industry, relevant ministries, educational institutions, and trade unions.

On the basis of the workshop, SAE developed a sector skills strategy defining priority actions at policy, institutional, and company level, as well as roles, responsibilities and institutional arrangements within the sector to accomplish envisaged actions. 
As a follow-up the SAE engaged with relevant institutions, including the Sector Skills Council for the hotel sector to promote the strategy document and discuss measures aimed at a better alignment of training provision with the needs of the industry. The ILO provided technical support throughout the entire skills needs anticipation exercise and drafting of the sector skills strategy. In particular, the ILO provided substantial inputs to the diagnostic background study, facilitated the skills foresight workshop, and reviewed and contributed to the sector skills strategy for the hotel industry. 

At the same time, the ILO introduced three SAE staff to STED methodology and provided further capacity building to one selected on measuring skills mismatches though training delivered by ILO ITC Turin in November 2022. Knowledge acquired through training significantly enlarged the perspective of how to address skills mismatches and proved to be instrumental in providing more qualified advice to members on the above-mentioned topic. The SAE was further engaged in all stages of the assessment improving their capacity to conduct independent analytical work in the future. 

Furthermore, the SAE was actively engaged in the communication campaign to promote the findings of the research and recommendations for addressing the skills needs deficit in the hotel industry. Under ILO guidance the SAE promoted the skills needs exercise via its network, while the communication agency helped in reaching media outlets and engaging their interest in the topic. The SAE was also advised on how to systematically engage with the policy makers and report back to members on the outcomes of these engagements.</narrative>
   </description>
